      "side": 1,
      "message": "Maybe we should check for `propName \u003d\u003d \"Src\"` first since this won\u0027t indicate an error condition, and then we can check that `propVal` has type `*string` which we can check and report an error if that\u0027s not the case. Might help prevent an unintentional regression if the type of `src` was to change in the future.\n\nAside: from a reader\u0027s perspective, I wasn\u0027t sure why we are checking that the `propVal` had type `*string` until I got later into the code block, but by switching the `if` statements around, I think that the context can be built a little more clearly which helps me scan the code and quickly understand what it is doing.\n\nE.g.\n\n```\nif propName \u003d\u003d \"Src\" {\n\tprops, ok :\u003d propVal.(*string)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\tctx.PropertyErrorf(\"src\", \"expected property to have type string, but was: %v\", propVal.(type))  // can\u0027t remember if Go lets you print the type like this, but some error messsage along these lines\n\t\tcontinue\n\t}\n\tlabel :\u003d android.BazelLabelForModuleSrcSingle(ctx, *props)\n\tsrc.SetSelectValue(configProp.ConfigurationAxis(), configProp.SelectKey(), label)\n}\t\t\t\n```",
